 { Earlier in the chapter, we saw how you can use the so-called 'Minority Attack' to inflict damage on the opponent's position. While it's true that a pawn minority can offer a way to pressure the opponent, the same can be said of a pawn majority. In this section, we'll be covering the most common (and dangerous!) pawn majority: the 4 vs 3 majority on the kingside. Let's jump in to our first example. } 1. { -KEY- } Nxd5 $1 { White initiates a series of piece exchanges with the goal of achieving a 4 vs 3 majority. } Bxd5 ( 1... exd5 $2 { would be worse, since here White still has the 4 vs 3 majority but in addition they would get to keep the superior light-squared bishop. } ) 
2. Bxd5 $1 { The second piece exchange forces the 4 vs 3 majority. } exd5 { Now White has built up the 4 vs 3 majority. Black does now have a 4 vs 3 majority on the queenside, but this one is not dangerous. The problem for Black is that their pawn majority is not facing the enemy king! } 3. e6 $1 { The 4 vs 3 majority immediately sets up the possibility of a pawn break. } Nb6 ( 3... fxe6 4. Qxe6 { White will take on d5 next and remain a clear pawn to the good. } ) 
4. dxc5 { Leaving Black with an isolated pawn on d5. } Bxc5 5. Ng5 f6 { The pressure against f7 forces Black's hand. Now White has a dangerous passed pawn on e6. } ( 5... fxe6 6. Qxe6 Kh8 7. Nf7 $18 ) 
6. Nf7 { Using the advanced outpost. } Qe7 7. Qg4 { Threatening to play Nf7-h6-f5. } Rfe8 { Pressuring the pawn on e6 and giving Black's king some breathing room. } 8. Re1 $18 { The passed pawn and the outposted knight are quite stable, leaving Black to constantly worry about devastating tactics against their king. Black is close to lost, for example: } Nc4 9. Bh6 g6 10. h4 { Setting up pawn break possibilities. } Nd6 { Finally Black manages to challenge the powerful knight. } 11. Nxd6 Bxd6 12. h5 $1 { The pawn break forces further weaknesses in the Black camp. } g5 13. g3 Rac8 14. Rad1 Rc4 15. Qf5 $16 { White still has some work to do in order to decisively break through. However, the pressure in the centre and kingside should be enough to win with best play. } 
