 { Can you find a sequence to force a kingside pawn majority? } 1. { -KEY- } cxd5 $1 { The first step, drawing out the e6 pawn to the d-file. } ( { The immediate attempt to gain space would fail after } 1. e5 $2 d4 $1 2. Qd2 Nd7 $19 { when the e5 pawn cannot be defended. } ) 
exd5 2. e5 $1 { Correct! By advancing the e-pawn, White ensures there will be no more contact between their e4 pawn and Black's d5 pawn, in effect 'activating' their majority. } Nh7 ( 2... d4 { Now this move doesn't work, since White has a clever tactical resource: } 3. Nexd4 cxd4 4. Qxd4 $16 { The double attack ensures that White will regain their piece on the next move, emerging a pawn to the good. } ) 
 ( 2... Nd7 $2 { would allow the same pawn break as in our first example after } 3. e6 $16 { The loss of Black's pawn on f7 will leave them with a permanently weak king. } ) 
3. d4 $1 { White is willing to give Black a protected passed pawn in exchange for stabilizing their central e5 pawn. } c4 { In the game Black took the opportunity to gain the protected passer, but it is very well blockaded and so White is firmly in command. From here the game can take many directions, but the key objective is to continue to advance the majority. } 4. Nh2 $1 b5 5. f4 Qd7 { Black tries for a pawn blockade. } 6. Rf1 Qf5 7. Qf2 $1 { White is in no rush to advance, instead choosing to first optimise their piece configuration. } ( { Advancing immediately with } 7. g4 { might allow more counterplay than ideal, after a sequence like } Qc2 8. f5 Nh4 $13 ) 
Ra8 { An attempt to generate counterplay. } 8. Bc3 $1 { White shuts down the queenside completely, before turning their focus onto mobilising the pawn majority on the kingside. } Bd8 9. g4 Qc2 { Now the same plan of invading on the queenside does not convince, as White is much better coordinated than in the variation where we saw this previously after 7.g4. } 10. Rbc1 Qa2 11. Nf3 { Covering the h4 square adds strength to the threat of f4-f5. Moreover, Black's queen on a2 is at risk of getting trapped. } hxg4 12. hxg4 $18 { Black cannot stop the threat of f4-f5, and once that move is played two advanced pawn breaks (f5-f6 or e5-e6) will open up for White. From here, not only will Black's minor pieces be extremely cramped but Black's king will be in serious danger, with mating patterns likely to begin to form soon. White is already winning. } 
