 { Sometimes it is not just about piece or pawn exchanges, and you will need to lean on other elements of the position to establish a 4 vs 3 majority. In this position we will see how White uses the presence of an outpost on d6 to their advantage. } 1. { -KEY- } Nxe5 $1 { White begins by capturing with the knight. This forces Black to recapture with the bishop. } ( 1. dxe5 $2 { This sloppy move loses the advantage. } Bg4 $1 { when suddenly things are not so clear, as Black will not have to part ways with their valuable dark-squared bishop in order to recover the central pawn on e5. } ) 
Bxe5 2. dxe5 Qxe5 { The first part of the operation is complete. While it's true that White has forced Black to part with the dark-squared bishop, at first glance this is not a major issue since they had the bishop pair to begin with. } 3. e4 $1 { Unfortunately for Black, they overlooked this key pawn break. Under normal circumstances, Black would simply exchange on e4 and be left with a symmetrical pawn structure and approximate equality. Here, however, they don't have that luxury. } d4 { Black is left with no choice but to advance the pawn. However, the problem with this is that now White is left with the 4 vs 3 kingside pawn majority, which they are able to immediately mobilise. } ( 3... dxe4 $2 4. Nxe4 $1 { The exchange of pawns has transformed the outpost on d6 from a square that could not be reached, to one that is about to be permanently occupied. The knight on d6 will be far too strong for Black to be able to hold together the defense. For example: } Be6 5. Nd6 Re7 6. b5 $18 { The Black queenside is about to collapse. } ) 
4. f4 $1 { Advancing the majority. } Qg7 5. Nb1 $1 { White identifies the ideal route for the knight. } Be6 6. Qd3 $1 { Blockading the passed pawn, which is the price that White had to pay in order to obtain the 4 vs 3 majority. } a6 7. Nd2 Rad8 8. e5 $1 { Grabbing space, shutting the Black queen out of the game, and also creating a power outpost on d6. } Re7 9. Ne4 Bf5 { Black has to pin the knight as if it reaches the d6 square it's lights out. } 10. Rc4 $16 { White is firmly in command of the position, with Black's d4 pawn unlikely to survive in the long run. } 
