 { Until now, we've discussed the importance of mobilising the majority on a number of occasions. However, your opponents will often be aware of your plan and seek to counter them. Because of this, a strong sense of timing is key. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxf5 $1 { White rightly hurries to capture the bishop in the online game Carlsen,M - Bortnyk,O (2023). } Nxf5 2. g4 $1 { Anything else and Black would equalize. } ( 2. Ne2 $2 { This move, or practically any other, would be met by the 'automatic' reply of } h4 $11 { when suddenly Black secures the outpost on g3 and renders White's g2 pawn immobile. As a result, the pawn majority loses its strength. } ) 
Ne7 3. f5 $1 { White had achieved his goal and went on to win the game. Future pawn breaks in the form of f5-f6 or e5-e6 are possible, while already Black's minor pieces are feeling the effect of the lack of space. It is already not much of an exaggeration to say that White is winning. Let's show one way in which play might continue: } O-O-O 4. Ne2 $1 Nd7 5. Nf4 hxg4 6. hxg4 a6 7. Rhf1 { Stacking both rooks behind the advanced pawns. } Nc5 8. f6 { The decisive pawn break. } gxf6 9. exf6 Ng8 10. Ne5 { The f7 pawn is awkward to defend. } Rh7 11. g5 { The presence of advanced connected pawns spells tactical doom for Black, since now possibilities revolving around sacrificing a knight for the f7 pawn are certain to work. One beautiful continuation is as follows: } Rf8 12. Nfg6 $1 Rd8 { Black declines the sacrifice. } ( 12... fxg6 { is hopeless. After } 13. Nxg6 Rd8 14. f7 $18 { Black is forced to give up a full rook to stop the pawn. } ) 
13. Nh8 $3 { A beautifully creative finish! } Rxh8 14. Nxf7 { White will grab one of the rooks next, when material will be roughly equal while White's connected passed pawns will soon force resignation. Until now all of the examples that we've shown have presented the 4 vs 3 pawn majority as the holy grail of positional assets! While it's true that it is usually a significant advantage, that is not always the case. In our next example we'll show a case where the majority offers no real benefit. } 
