 { In this position White has a 4 vs 3 kingside majority. However, the absence of minor pieces, coupled with Black's control over the d-file, takes away all of its sting. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 { White tries to advance. } Qd4+ { Simplest, but even a quiet move like ...Kf8 would have held the balance. } 2. Qxd4 Rxd4 3. e6 $2 { White tries to advance the pawns further. Of course, Black will not allow them to run towards the finish line! } ( { White should not get overly ambitious, and instead go for a move such as this rook lift, in order to be able to eye up the queenside. } 3. Re3 $11 ) 
Kf8 $1 { By avoiding ...fxe6, Black could have set up some practical chances to complicate matters for White. } ( 3... fxe6 $11 { This move was played in the game, but here it leads to a very easy draw due to forced simplifications: } 4. fxe6 Kf8 5. Rf1 $1 Ke7 6. Rf7 Kxe6 7. Rxb7 $11 ) 
4. exf7 Kxf7 { Suddenly the f5 pawn is a bit vulnerable. } 5. Re3 Rd1+ 6. Kh2 Rd5 7. Rf3 ( { If } 7. g4 Rd2 $17 { picks up the b2 pawn. } ) 
Kf6 { White cannot avoid losing a pawn. With best play they may still be able to find drawing opportunities, but in practical terms a very unpleasant defense lies ahead. This game highlights that a pawn majority tends to lose its strength when there are fewer pieces on the board. It's worth keeping this in mind as either the attacking or defending side. While some 4 vs 3 majorities are not dangerous, many of them are. For this reason the best cure is to prevent it from forming in the first place! Let's show a couple of examples of this next. } 
