 { Black is doing fine here in an online game against Magnus Carlsen. However, with his next move he needlessly concedes the pawn majority. } 1... { -KEY- } d3 $2 { Black offers the exchange of the d-pawn for White's pawn on b2. This is a bad exchange as Black cannot hope to make use of the 2 vs 1 majority on the queenside, while White's 4 vs 3 kingside majority will prove extremely dangerous. } ( { Black was fine, and a move such as } 1... Re8 $1 { would have maintained the very drawish nature of this opposite colored bishop middlegame. } ) 
2. Qxd3 Bxb2 3. Rc2 $1 { In the game Magnus went wrong with Rc1-d1, but this move is much stronger in that it keeps up the pressure against Black's position by immediately targeting the Black bishop. } Be5 4. Qd5 { Black is tied up. His queen defends the b7 pawn, while his rook is needed to defend the f7 pawn. At first glance this would not seem like a major problem, perhaps Black can simply shuffle his bishop back and forth along the a1-h8 diagonal? } Bf6 5. e4 $1 { Aha! This is the problem. White immediately activates his pawn majority. } Bd4 6. Rd2 ( 6. e5 $6 Qd8 $1 { is messy. } ) 
Bc3 7. Rd3 Bf6 8. e5 $1 { Finally the bishop is kicked away from the diagonal. } Bd8 9. Qd7 $1 { The pawn majority leads to a painful bind, and soon White will target the paralysed f7 pawn. } Qc6+ { The queen exchange will not save Black. } 10. Qxc6 bxc6 11. Rd7 $1 Bb6 12. f4 $1 { White's superior structure and piece placement kills any hope of a defense for Black. Black could try to remain passive here, but there is a well-studied method for converting such positions involving multiple pawn breaks. Let's cover it now as it is a technique worth being familiar with. } Kg8 13. Kf3 Kg7 14. g4 $1 { The first pawn break. } hxg4+ 15. Kxg4 Kg8 16. h5 $1 { The second pawn break. } gxh5+ 17. Kxh5 { White has converted their 4 vs 3 majority into a 2 vs 1 majority and at the same time have turned their king into an attacking piece. } Kg7 18. f5 Rh8+ 19. Kg4 Rf8 20. f6+ $1 ( 20. e6 $6 { Advancing the pawns on the same color square as your bishop is a risky approach in opposite colored bishop endgames (due to the possibility of a blockade on the opposite color). Although here White's advantage is so great that the position would still be winning. } ) 
Kg8 ( 20... Kg6 { There are many ways for White to win against this move. Here is one possibility: } 21. Bc2 Kh6 22. Kf5 $18 { With the king cut off on h6, White will soon capture the f7 pawn. } ) 
21. Kh5 Bc5 22. e6 $1 { The third, and decisive, pawn break. } fxe6 23. Bxe6+ Kh8 24. Kg6 $18 { Black has no good way to stop the threat of mate with Rh7#. This example was quite advanced, but a good illustration of the power of the 4 vs 3 majority. Once conceded, Black was unable to make a draw in spite of the reduced material and the presence of opposite colored bishops. } 
