 ( { If you had to recapture on d5 with a pawn, which do you think is best between } 1. Kg6 exd5 { and } cxd5 $2 ) 
 { -KEY- } cxd5 $1 { Correct! By capturing in this way, Black avoids conceding a 4 vs 3 majority. The position is roughly equal. } ( 1... exd5 $2 { In the game Black mistakenly recaptured with the e-pawn, allowing a 4 vs 3 formation. } 2. Bd3 { White offers a bishop exchange. This makes sense both because this is Black's most active piece, but also because eventually White will wish to expand with f2-f4-f5 and so it is important to clear any resistance along the light squares. } Bxd3 3. Qxd3 Nb6 $2 { Making matters worse. A move like ...g6 instead would have strengthened control over the f5 square, preparing for the impending pawn storm. } 4. O-O Qd7 5. Nh4 $1 { Controlling the f5 square and clearing the path for the f2 pawn. } O-O-O 6. f4 $1 { Even in the absence of Black's king on the kingside, the pawn advance is highly dangerous. } Ng6 { Black is hoping for Nxg6? when after ...hxg6 they will have gained some control over the f5 square. } 7. Nf3 $1 { Instead, White rejects the piece exchange and maintains the threat of f4-f5 on the next move. } f5 { Black feels compelled to stop f4-f5, but now White is left with a protected passed pawn on e5. } 8. a4 $16 { White might now shift their attention to the queenside, enjoying the luxury of being able to play on all sides of the board. Black lacks a plan, and is dangerously close to being objectively lost. } ) 
2. Bd3 Bxd3 3. Qxd3 Nc6 4. O-O Be7 $11 { Black will castle next. White's space advantage is not significant since all of Black's pieces have good squares. } 
