 { We'll finish the section with an instructive sequence played by Magnus Carlsen with the White pieces. We mentioned earlier that pawn majorities tend to lose strength as pieces come off the board, but the flip side of this is that in endgames the possibility of creating a passed pawn can become even more dangerous. Here, this will prove to be decisive. } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 Kc6 2. h4 $1 { White is ready to break with g4-g5 next. } ( { But not } 2. Bxd6 $2 { since after } cxd6 $11 { Black not only corrects their structure, but by shifting the pawn from c7 to d6, they are now able to neutralize White's 4 vs 3 majority. This is because the d6 pawn is able to influence, or make contact with, the White e4 pawn if it tries to advance. } ) 
b5 $2 { Black tries to mobilize their own pawn majority on the queenside. The problem he faces however is that not only are his pawns much less advanced, but because they are doubled on the c-file, they cannot be easily converted into a passed pawn. } ( 2... h6 $1 { This would have offered greater defensive chances for Black, as now g4-g5 becomes less attractive due to the consequent opening of the h-file: } 3. g5 $2 { White would have been better off playing 3.Rh1, controlling the h-file before opening lines on the kingside. } hxg5 4. hxg5 Rh8 $13 { Black's active counterplay leaves them with good practical chances to hold. } ) 
3. g5 $1 { White is already winning. } Be7 4. Rg1 { The patient approach. There is no need for White to take any risk of miscalculating a forcing line like the one just shown. Instead, White prepares for the opening up of the g-file at the right moment. } ( 4. gxf6 { This is the most direct approach, but Magnus understandably preferred to avoid concrete calculation as in such cases there is always the risk of an oversight. } Bxf6 5. e5 { should also be enough to win, with the point being that ...Bxe5 is met by Re1, pinning the bishop. For example: } Bxe5 6. Re1 Kd6 7. Rxe5 Rxe5 8. c4 $18 { The c4 and f5 pawns combine to rob Black of either the e6 or d5 squares. As a result, Black will eventually find himself in zugzwang and his king will be forced to abandon the defense of the rook. } ) 
a5 5. h5 c4 ( 5... fxg5 { Capturing on g5 will not change Black's fate. } 6. Bxg5 Bxg5 7. Rxg5 Re7 8. h6 $1 { The cleanest path to converting. The point is that now White will establish connected passed pawns on the e- and f-files. } gxh6 9. Rh5 $18 { White is winnning. } ) 
6. h6 $1 { By targeting the base of Black's pawn structure, White forces connected passed pawns. } cxb3 7. cxb3 fxg5 ( 7... gxh6 8. gxf6 Bxf6 { is also hopeless, with the easiest path to victory being } 9. Rc1 Kb7 10. Rxc7 Kb6 11. Rxh7 $18 { White is winning. } ) 
8. hxg7 $1 { Other moves also win, but Magnus spots that there is no need to worry about his bishop being under attack. } h6 ( 8... gxf4 9. g8=Q $18 ) 
9. e5 $18 { Black resigned, since the three connected passed pawns are unstoppable. In the next (and final!) section of the chapter, we'll explore various strategies for managing positions focused on specific pawn types, such as isolated, backward, and passed pawns, among others. Let's dive in! } 
